2. The game is played between two players, "Player 1" and "Player 2".
3. Each player has three options: Rock, Paper, or Scissors.
4. The game is won by the player who selects an option that the other player does not have.
5. The game is played until one player wins.

Here is a complete Java solution to the problem:
```
import java.util.Scanner;

public class RockPaperScissors {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
    System.out.println("Welcome to Rock, Paper, Scissors!");

    // Loop until a player wins
    while (true) {
      // Ask Player 1 to select an option
      System.out.println("Player 1, please select an option: Rock, Paper, or Scissors");
      String player1Option = scanner.nextLine();

      // Ask Player 2 to select an option
      System.out.println("Player 2, please select an option: Rock, Paper, or Scissors");
      String player2Option = scanner.nextLine();

      // Check if Player 1 and Player 2 have the same option
      if (player1Option.equals(player2Option)) {
        System.out.println("It's a tie!");
        continue;
      }

      // Ask Player 1 if they want to play again
      System.out.println("Player 1, do you want to play again? (yes/no)");
      String player1Next = scanner.nextLine();

      // If Player 1 wants to play again, loop back to the beginning
      if (player1Next.equalsIgnoreCase("yes")) {
        System.out.println("Player 1, please select an option: Rock, Paper, or Scissors");
        continue;
      }

      // If Player 1 does not want to play again, Player 2 wins
      System.out.println("Player 2 wins!");
      break;
    }
  }
}
```
